00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.960
Okay, let’s now dive into the family&nbsp;
tree of birds. As you probably know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:29.440
birds are literally living dinosaurs (which means&nbsp;
that they are also technically reptiles—although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:35.040
they are generally classified separately as they&nbsp;
differ considerably from all other surviving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:41.120
reptiles). When it comes to dinosaurs, they can&nbsp;
basically be divided into two main categories: the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:48.880
now extinct Ornithischia (meaning bird-hipped; one&nbsp;
example being triceratops) and Saurischia (meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:54.640
reptile-hipped). Ironically, birds actually&nbsp;
descend from the reptile-hipped dinosaurs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.640 --> 00:03:00.960
which can be further divided into Theropods&nbsp;
(which once included the mighty T. Rex) and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:08.880
now extinct Sauropods (which included the largest&nbsp;
land animals ever, such as the arachiosaurus).

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:15.280
During the Age of Dinosaurs, there was a type of&nbsp;
theropod called the Paraves, the most well-known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:22.080
of which is the now extinct Archaeopteryx. Well,&nbsp;
the Aves Class (which includes all modern birds)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:29.360
also descends from and is still considered a&nbsp;
part the Paraves clayde. So we’re now at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:35.360
start of the Birds section, which is everything&nbsp;
shown in yellow, orange, or red. The word aves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:42.160
is simply the Latin word for bird and from here,&nbsp;
things go in three different directions. There’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:51.600
the Palaeognathae, the Galloanserae, and the&nbsp;
Neoaves. The word “Palaeognathae” simply means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:58.240
“old jaws” and was chosen because the birds&nbsp;
in this group are anatomically closer to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:04.800
extinct dinosaurs (especially in the beak and&nbsp;
sternum area) as compared to other modern birds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:10.000
They are also notable in that most of them&nbsp;
have lost the ability to fly, such as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:18.080
ostriches in Africa, rheas in South America,&nbsp;
emus in Australia, and kiwis in New Zealand.

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:25.280
The word Galloanserae is simply a combination&nbsp;
of Gallo (which is Latin for rooster) and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:31.920
anserem (which is Latin for goose). Birds in this&nbsp;
clayde are known as fowl and belong to either the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:39.200
waterfowl order (called Anseriformes, after the&nbsp;
word for goose) or land fowl (called Galliformes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:43.840
after the word for rooster). Aside&nbsp;
from quail (who have their own family),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:48.400
most of the familiar types of land&nbsp;
fowl belong to the Phasianidae,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:55.280
or pheasant family. In addition to pheasants,&nbsp;
this family also includes grouse, partridges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.280 --> 00:05:01.840
turkeys, and of course, chickens (all of which are&nbsp;
types of birds commonly eaten by humans). However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:07.040
it also includes peafowl, which are&nbsp;
rarely eaten but instead admired for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:12.320
their beautiful feathers. Take note that the&nbsp;
word peacock only refers to the male members&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:18.240
of the species and when referring to a female,&nbsp;
the word is actually pea-hen. When it comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:23.440
waterfowl, most are in the [uh-NAHT-uh-dee]&nbsp;
Anatidea, or duck family, which of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:31.280
includes ducks (such as the famous mallard)&nbsp;
as well as swans, geese, and Canada geese.

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:38.320
But the vast majority of bird species (about&nbsp;
95%) belong to the Neoaves (which literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:45.360
means “new birds”), hence the switch of color to&nbsp;
orange here. Now, just two 2 years ago, in 2024,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:51.280
the way that Neo-a-ves were classified was&nbsp;
changed based on a major genetic study. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:56.400
is now thought that they can be divided into&nbsp;
four main claydes: the Mirandornithes (which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:03.680
is basically just flamingoes and grebes, the&nbsp;
Columbaves (named after the Latin word for dove),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:10.480
Elementaves (after the word “element”)&nbsp;
and Telluraves (which means “land birds”).

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:17.680
Let’s start with the Columbaves, most of which&nbsp;
belong to the Columbiformes Order and Columbidae&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:23.760
Family (the main exception being the cuckoo birds&nbsp;
and road runners (which have their own order and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:30.160
family). So, in this family, we of course get&nbsp;
doves, such as collared doves and turtle doves as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:36.960
well as mourning doves. But we also get pigeons,&nbsp;
such as the domestic (or feral) pigeon that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:43.600
found in city parks and which descends from the&nbsp;
wild rock dove. Now, prior to the 20th century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:49.760
a different pigeon (known as the passenger pigeon)&nbsp;
was actually far more common in North America.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:56.560
However, due to overhunting and deforestation,&nbsp;
they went from a population of 4 billion to being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.560 --> 00:07:03.920
extinct in less than 100 years, with the last&nbsp;
passenger pigeon dying in 1914. But don’t get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:09.520
the passenger pigeon confused with the carrier&nbsp;
(or homing) pigeon, the kind that delivers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:15.920
messages. They are actually just specially trained&nbsp;
domestic pigeons and are still around to this day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:22.000
Also in this family is perhaps the most&nbsp;
famous extinct animal of all time—the dodo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:28.800
which lived on the remote island of Mauritius,&nbsp;
in the Indian Ocean. Because it had no natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:34.640
predators and lots of food, it evolved to the&nbsp;
point where it lost its ability to fly. Thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:42.240
when humans showed up with dogs and other animals,&nbsp;
they quickly went extinct, probably by 1690.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:47.600
Okay, let’s now look at the Elementaves,&nbsp;
so named because they can be found in many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:55.360
different environments or elements—such at sea,&nbsp;
on land, or along the shore. All shorebirds belong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:04.080
to the Charadriiformes Order, which includes the&nbsp;
plover family, the sandpiper family, and the gull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:11.280
family (which also includes terns and skimmers).&nbsp;
Now true seabirds (which are placed in a clayde&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:17.440
called Aque-ornithes) differ from shorebirds in&nbsp;
that they spend most of their time actually in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:24.480
the water (rather than just alongside it). So,&nbsp;
here we find loons (aka "stupid Canadian wolf&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:33.120
birds”) which btw are NOT ducks, even though they&nbsp;
look like ducks. We also get storks, cormorants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:39.920
and penguins (yet another type of bird that has&nbsp;
lost its ability to fly) as well as all the birds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:45.680
in the Pelican Order, many of which have evolved&nbsp;
away from the sea and into freshwater areas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:50.560
such as certain herons, and [ai-buses] ibises.&nbsp;
Now, one other type of bird that you might have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:57.120
assumed we would have come across in either the&nbsp;
seabird or shorebird category would be cranes. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:03.680
they’re almost exclusively freshwater birds (found&nbsp;
mainly in marshes)—which is why they’re placed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:09.840
separately over here. A lot of people confuse&nbsp;
cranes with herons so one way to tell them apart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:17.600
is that cranes fly with their necks stretched out&nbsp;
whereas herons fly with their necks in an S shape.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:25.280
One last order that is unexpectedly included&nbsp;
among the Elementaves is the Apodiformes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:30.560
which includes swifts and hummingbirds.&nbsp;
What sets them apart from other birds is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:38.880
their extremely short legs. You see, pod meets&nbsp;
foot so a-pod literally means “without feet”.

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:45.840
Okay, we’re now ready to tackle the final (and&nbsp;
by far the largest) type of Neo-a-ves: the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:53.200
Telluraves, or core land birds. But before I do&nbsp;
so I want to point out that most of the individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:59.920
bird images on this chart were taken from the&nbsp;
citizen science website iNaturalist (but only from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:06.560
observations with photos marked public domain).&nbsp;
In case you're unfamiliar with iNaturalist, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:13.520
has an app that can be used to identify and make&nbsp;
a record of nature observations (not just birds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:19.680
but also plants, mushrooms, insects, et cetera).&nbsp;
This video is not sponsored by iNaturalist but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:25.120
I wanted to bring it up in case there are any&nbsp;
other inatters out there who’d like to help by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:31.280
either contributing photos or suggesting things to&nbsp;
include. You can find me on iNaturalist under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:38.560
username matt hyphen b so feel free to message&nbsp;
me there or tag me in specific observations.

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:44.800
Okay, let's now take a look at the Telluraves.&nbsp;
Since 2012, this clayde has been further divided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:51.120
into two groups, based on a split that occurred&nbsp;
around the time of the KT extinction event.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:57.360
There's the Afroaves, which evolved on&nbsp;
what became Africa; and the Australaves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:02.800
which evolved on what became South&nbsp;
America and Australia. In Afro-a-ves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:11.760
we get the two main raptor (or birds of prey)&nbsp;
orders: Accipiteriformes and Strigiformes. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:16.880
[ak-sip-uh-tree FOR-meez] Accipiteriformes,&nbsp;
we get the osprey family and the hawk family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:22.640
(which includes not only hawks but also&nbsp;
eagles and buzzards). In Strigiformes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:29.360
we get all the various type of owls. Barn owls&nbsp;
get their own family called Tytonidae whereas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:36.160
most other owls (such as horned owls, snowy owls,&nbsp;
and wood owls) are found in the Strigidae family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:43.200
Take note that barred owls (not to be confused&nbsp;
with barn owls) are a type of wood owl. Also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:49.600
in the Afroaves category is the scavenger&nbsp;
Cathartiformes Order (which includes turkey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:57.040
vultures and condors) as well as kingfishers&nbsp;
and woodpeckers. Woodpeckers are placed in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:04.400
Picidae family and here, I’ve chosen to show the&nbsp;
four most common types of woodpeckers in the US.

00:12:04.400 --> 00:12:10.880
Moving over to the Australaves, we find the&nbsp;
falcons—the other major type of raptor. It used to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:17.680
be that hawks, eagles and falcons were all placed&nbsp;
in one big order but now, due to genetic studies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:23.680
we know that falcons evolved from a totally&nbsp;
different branch. In fact, they are more closely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:30.960
related to parrots than they are to hawks. All&nbsp;
parrots can be found in the Psittaciformes Order,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:38.080
where cockatoos get their own family and where&nbsp;
macaws, grey parrots, and Amazon parrots are all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:44.160
placed together in the main parrot family. That&nbsp;
family (the Psittacidae) should not be confused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:51.520
with this similarly named Psittaculidae family,&nbsp;
which is where budgies and lovebirds are placed.

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:56.480
Okay, we now come to the most important&nbsp;
order found within the Austral-a-ves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.480 --> 00:13:01.840
if not the entire Av-es Class: the&nbsp;
Passeriformes—which is where we find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.840 --> 00:13:09.440
about 60% of all bird species. This order is so&nbsp;
large that I decided to give them their own color:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:16.160
red. Now the name Passeriformes is based on&nbsp;
the Latin word for sparrow, which is Passer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.160 --> 00:13:21.840
and indeed, all birds in this order kind of look&nbsp;
like sparrows. But more importantly, they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:28.720
all “perching” birds, which means that they can&nbsp;
easily grab hold of and perch on branches. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.720 --> 00:13:35.040
is because their feet evolved to have three long&nbsp;
toes in the front and one long toe in the back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.040 --> 00:13:40.240
which makes wrapping them around a branch&nbsp;
really easy. Now, most Passerines (which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:46.320
is what we call birds in this order) are also&nbsp;
songbirds but a few are not, so let me point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:52.640
them out first. We’ve got the New Zealand wrens&nbsp;
in this very small suborder as well as the New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.640 --> 00:13:59.600
World flycatchers in the Tyranni suborder, which&nbsp;
is why they’re also called tyrant flycatchers.

00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:05.760
Okay, let’s now talk about songbirds, which are&nbsp;
all birds belonging to the Passeri Suborder.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:11.840
Songbirds (also known as Oscines) differ from&nbsp;
all other birds in that they have several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:16.560
extra muscles in their windpipes and these&nbsp;
allow them to produce more complex sounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:22.720
which they use to pass on information and&nbsp;
to attract mates. The Passeri suborder can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:30.160
be divided into two infraorders: the Passerides&nbsp;
infraorder (which includes most songbirds) and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:37.200
the Corvides infraorder, which is where we find&nbsp;
corvids. Now, corvids are special because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:42.560
are the smartest birds on the planet, with an&nbsp;
intelligence level roughly equivalent to that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:48.720
of a 7 year old child. While they cannot&nbsp;
mimic human speech as well as parrots can,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:55.200
they are able to solve more complex puzzles,&nbsp;
build tools out of multiple parts, and remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.200 --> 00:15:01.840
human faces over many years. Included in&nbsp;
this family are jackdaws, magpies, jays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.840 --> 00:15:09.520
rooks, ravens, and of course, crows. In most of&nbsp;
North America, there is just one crow species:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.520 --> 00:15:15.040
Corvus brachyrhynchos (which literally&nbsp;
means “short-nosed crow). However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.040 --> 00:15:22.880
in Europe, there are at least two: the all-black&nbsp;
carrion crow and the black-and-grey hooded crow.

00:15:22.880 --> 00:15:28.880
Right. Let’s now move order to Passerides&nbsp;
infraorder, which we’re once again going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.880 --> 00:15:36.080
divide up, this time into three parvorders.&nbsp;
First there’s theSylviidae parvorder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:42.560
which is where we find the Paridae (or “tit”&nbsp;
family). There’s no need to giggle—the word&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:48.240
simply comes from an old Scandinavian word&nbsp;
that means “small”. And indeed, this family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:53.440
does include some of the smallest songbirds&nbsp;
(such as the familiar chickadee). However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.440 --> 00:15:59.920
take note that bushtits (which are even smaller)&nbsp;
are placed in their own family. Also closely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:05.760
related are the Old World warblers, larks, and&nbsp;
swallows. Now, I should pause for a minute here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:12.320
and explain the terms Old World and New World.&nbsp;
Old World refers to Europe, Africa, and Asia;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.320 --> 00:16:20.000
whereas New World refers to the Americas. These&nbsp;
are obviously terms based on colonization and are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:26.240
therefore problematic but unfortunately they&nbsp;
are still the main terms used in taxonomy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.240 --> 00:16:31.920
The important thing to understand is that there&nbsp;
are often two different versions of many types&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:37.520
of birds (such as warblers). Basically,&nbsp;
when the Europeans came to the Americas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:42.640
they saw some birds that looked to them like the&nbsp;
warblers they had back home—and therefore they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:48.560
called them “warblers” (even though we now know&nbsp;
that they’re not actually closely related). We’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.560 --> 00:16:54.880
going to see this come up again with sparrows,&nbsp;
robins, finches, and a bunch of other birds).

00:16:54.880 --> 00:17:02.320
Next up is the Muscicapida parvorder, named after&nbsp;
the Muscicapidae family, which literally means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.320 --> 00:17:09.760
“fly-catching” family. Here we find the European&nbsp;
robin, which is a type of flycatcher. But also in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:16.640
this parvorder are the thrushes, which are not&nbsp;
flycatchers and instead belong to the Turdidae&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:23.760
family. This is where we find the red-breasted&nbsp;
American robin, as well as the Eurasian blackbird,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:33.360
plus all the various species of bluebirds. Also&nbsp;
in Muscicapida are mockingbids, wrens, nuthatches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.360 --> 00:17:40.400
kinglets, and starlings. Starlings are notable in&nbsp;
that they are sometimes flock together to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.400 --> 00:17:47.360
large murmurations. However, in North America,&nbsp;
they are often considered pests because they don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.360 --> 00:17:53.840
belong here and can end up outcompeting native&nbsp;
birds. You see, originally, starlings only lived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:59.680
in Eurasia but then, in the late 1800s, a small&nbsp;
number of them were released in New York’s Central&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:59.680 --> 00:18:07.360
Park because, well, it seemed like a nice idea at&nbsp;
the time. Unfortunately, there are now about 100&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.360 --> 00:18:14.160
million of them in North America, and like I say,&nbsp;
they often outcompete native birds so nowadays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.160 --> 00:18:20.000
starlings are mostly a lesson in what not&nbsp;
to do when it comes to wildlife management.

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:26.160
The final parvorder here is Passerida,&nbsp;
which is where we find the Passeridae&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.160 --> 00:18:32.640
(or Old World sparrow) family, which is the&nbsp;
type of bird that the entire Passeriformes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.640 --> 00:18:38.320
Order is named after. The sparrow shown here&nbsp;
is a House Sparrow, which, like the Starling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.320 --> 00:18:43.840
was originally just a Eurasian species&nbsp;
but was then introduced to North America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.840 --> 00:18:52.000
where it has since become invasive. However, also&nbsp;
in the same parvorder is the or finch family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:57.520
which includes both Old World and New World&nbsp;
species. Finches are different from other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.520 --> 00:19:03.920
songbirds in that they tend to have short, stout&nbsp;
beaks that are really good at breaking nuts. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.920 --> 00:19:10.160
in this family, we get the chaeff-inch, this&nbsp;
goldfinch (both from Europe) as well as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.160 --> 00:19:17.120
house finch and this goldfinch (both from&nbsp;
North America). Then there’s the canary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:22.960
which was originally from Europe but has since&nbsp;
been domesticated and sold all over the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.960 --> 00:19:30.720
as pets. In the US, they were also infamously&nbsp;
sent into coal mines to detect carbon dioxide.

00:19:30.720 --> 00:19:36.480
The final set of birds were going to look at all&nbsp;
come from the Americas and are known as New World&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.480 --> 00:19:42.800
Nine-Primaried Oscines. “Nine-Primaried”&nbsp;
refers to fact that they have only 9 main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.800 --> 00:19:47.840
feathers on their wings (as opposed to&nbsp;
most other birds, which usually have 10,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.840 --> 00:19:52.960
just like we have 10 fingers). So, first of all,&nbsp;
there’s the Cardinal family, which of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.960 --> 00:19:59.040
includes the Northern Cardinal (probably the most&nbsp;
easily recognized bird in North America). However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.040 --> 00:20:05.680
it also includes certain grosbeaks, buntings, and&nbsp;
tanagers (although most “true” tanagers belong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:05.680 --> 00:20:12.000
to there own family, here). Another important&nbsp;
Nine-Primaried family is the Icteridae family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:16.800
which is where you’ll find grackles,&nbsp;
orioles (such as the Baltimore Oriole),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.800 --> 00:20:23.920
and the New World blackbirds. There’s also the New&nbsp;
World warbler family (which I pointed out earlier)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.920 --> 00:20:30.640
and then finally, the Passerellidae, or (New&nbsp;
World sparrow) family. Like I mentioned earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.640 --> 00:20:36.320
these sparrows aren’t as closely related to the&nbsp;
Old World sparrows as you might think, even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:36.320 --> 00:20:41.600
they are both called “sparrows”. European settlers&nbsp;
simply named them sparrows because they looked a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.600 --> 00:20:46.800
lot like the house sparrow that they were used&nbsp;
to back home. So, in contrast to the European&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:53.760
house sparrow, sparrows that are native to North&nbsp;
America include crowned sparrows, song sparrows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.760 --> 00:21:00.080
as well as towhees and juncos (even though those&nbsp;
last two aren’t directly named as being sparrows).

00:21:00.080 --> 00:21:06.800
Okay, so that was a look at the family tree of&nbsp;
birds. I’d love to hear in the comments which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.800 --> 00:21:12.080
bird species are the most common in your&nbsp;
own backyard. Here in Vancouver, Canada,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.080 --> 00:21:18.720
at my (and my wife’s) birdfeeders, we mostly&nbsp;
get house finches, goldfinches, chickadees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.720 --> 00:21:26.560
song sparrows, juncos, Northern flickers, Anna’s&nbsp;
hummingbirds, and of course American crows).

